  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">Up to the lookout</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The hike up was about four miles and 2,300 feet of climbing, just under two hours of moving time, crossing Sulphur Creek and Schriebers Meadow before climbing through old-growth forest into Morovitz Meadow. The junction with Railroad Grade, the alternate route toward Easton Glacier on Baker, was the part of the trail I'd never seen before, and it was stunning. There were a few creek crossings and a snow section that felt doable but sketchy. I should have brought microspikes, or at least poles.
  </p>

  <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 12px; max-width: 660px; margin: 20px 0;">
    <img loading="lazy"
      src="/images/park-butte/hike-up-selfie.jpg"
      alt="Selfie on the trail up to Park Butte"
      style="width: 100%; border-radius: 10px; object-fit: cover; aspect-ratio: 4/5;"
    />
    <img loading="lazy"
      src="/images/park-butte/trail-junction-sign.jpg"
      alt="Trail junction sign on the way up"
      style="width: 100%; border-radius: 10px; object-fit: cover; aspect-ratio: 4/5;"
    />
  </div>

  <img loading="lazy"
    src="/images/park-butte/snow-field-hike.jpg"
    alt="Wide snow field on the hike up with Baker in the background"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 660px; border-radius: 10px; margin: 0 0 20px 0;"
  />

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I made it to the lookout at 7pm. It was just me. Gorgeous, and quiet in the specific way a place gets when you know you're the only person in it for the night. I ate snacks, journaled by hand on the porch, and just soaked in the view, watching the light change over the peaks. By 8:12 it was still light out and I was already pooped.
  </p>

  <img loading="lazy"
    src="/images/park-butte/lookout-journal.jpg"
    alt="Journal open on the lookout table, Baker visible through the window"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 480px; border-radius: 10px; margin: 12px 0 20px 0;"
  />

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    At 8:30, three teenagers showed up to make vlogs, which broke the idyllic solitude I'd been so proud of. It turned out to be a good thing. Sleeping up there completely alone, with the wind working on the shutters all night, would have been scary without them. I fell asleep soon after.
  </p>

