    src="/images/park-butte/descent-snow-field.jpg"
    alt="Descending the snow field in the morning with Baker and surrounding peaks"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 660px; border-radius: 10px; margin: 0 0 20px 0;"
  />

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    <a href="https://www.strava.com/activities/18951148087">My Strava caption</a> for that one says it best: "2 hrs lookout to car but 10++++ car to home." I wasn't exaggerating enough.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">The pothole that wasn't</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I started driving out at 8:11. At 8:23, two miles down the same gravel road I'd come in on, something slammed underneath the car and the tire blew. I told a friend it was a pothole, because that's what it felt like. I had no cell signal out there — none. The only thing that worked was Apple Emergency SOS satellite texting, which I had never used before and had no idea would actually work until I sent the first message and it went through.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I texted my brother. He was on the other side of the country. He coordinated from there — working through where exactly I was, what I had, and whether AAA could even dispatch to a forest road with no address. My dad, watching this unfold from India, called in to help sort out the membership details. Between the two of them, they figured it out.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    While I waited, an older couple came down the road and stopped. They gave me snacks and let me borrow their portable battery pack to charge my phone. A little later, an Alpine Ascents group came through — the same company I'd climbed Baker with two years earlier — and let me use their satellite phone. Eight hours on a gravel road in the North Cascades, and people kept stopping to help.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.04); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
    4:30a — up for sunrise<br/>
    6a — started hiking down<br/>
    8a — at car, on schedule<br/>
    8:11 — driving out<br/>
    8:23 — something slammed under the car<br/>
    3pm — tow truck
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">The tow</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The tow truck picked me up at 3pm for a 98-mile haul from the trailhead to Autologic in Bellevue. I'd already missed the noon and 1:30 calls. When a friend asked if I might still make the 6:30 flight, I told her I wasn't making it — I was still way up the gravel road with the tow. I dropped the car at Autologic that evening. I was physically fine, great even. The car was not.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    While I was sitting in the tow truck cab somewhere on I-5, my Garmin buzzed.
  </p>

  <img loading="lazy"
    src="/images/park-butte/tow-truck-garmin.jpg"
    alt="Garmin watch in the tow truck cab showing 'Fitness Age Decreased: 29.5'"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 480px; border-radius: 10px; margin: 12px 0;"
  />

  <img loading="lazy"
    src="/images/park-butte/tow-truck-tamales.jpg"
    alt="Eating walking tamales in the tow truck on the highway back to Bellevue"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 600px; border-radius: 10px; margin: 0 0 20px 0;"
  />

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">What actually happened</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    In January, I'd had the rear brakes serviced and hadn't thought about it since. I'd only put about 2,000 miles on the car between then and June. At the time, I assumed it was on me: driving too boldly on a potholed gravel road, hitting something wrong. It wasn't until days later, on calls with the shop, that I found out how much more serious it actually was. The left rear brake caliper had come off the car entirely while I was driving through the mountains, and it was the caliper, dangling and dragging, that punctured the tire. A very unfortunate miss in the repair — and the shop from January had the car towed from Autologic to sort it out.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">A little of the evening back</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I still didn't have real cell service by late afternoon, so a friend called the shop for me to sort out the drop-off. It turned out nobody was in a rush. The shop was fine with me leaving the key if I got there after close, which meant I still had a night ahead of me instead of losing it entirely.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I went home, ate takeout, showered, put on compression boots, and went to bed. I decided I wasn't fixing the car before I left. It would happen. It felt surreal to have gone from the most beautiful sunrise of my life, to languishing on a mountain for hours, and then just ending back in my bed — and 12 hours later being at the airport to meet my family for a different kind of adventure.
  </p>

